{"index": 0, "header": "import Mathlib\n\nopen Complex Filter Function Metric Finset\nopen scoped BigOperators Topology\n\n", "code": "import Mathlib\n\nopen Complex Filter Function Metric Finset\nopen scoped BigOperators Topology\n\n/-- Suppose that $f$ is holomorphic in an open set $\\Omega$. Prove that if $\\text{Im}(f)$ is constant, then $f$ is constant.-/\ntheorem exercise_1_13b {f : ℂ → ℂ} (Ω : Set ℂ) (a b : Ω) (h : IsOpen Ω)\n  (hf : DifferentiableOn ℂ f Ω) (hc : ∃ (c : ℝ), ∀ z ∈ Ω, (f z).im = c) :\n  f a = f b :=  by\n  /-\n  Given that \\( f \\) is a holomorphic function in an open set \\( \\Omega \\) and the imaginary part of \\( f \\) is constant on \\( \\Omega \\), we need to prove that \\( f \\) is constant on \\( \\Omega \\). Since the imaginary part of \\( f \\) is constant, there exists a real number \\( c \\) such that for all \\( z \\) in \\( \\Omega \\), the imaginary part of \\( f(z) \\) is equal to \\( c \\). By the properties of complex numbers, the equality of the imaginary parts implies that \\( f \\) must be constant because the function's behavior in the real and imaginary parts are constrained to be constant.\n  -/\n  -- We start by obtaining the constant value of the imaginary part of f.\n  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := hc\n  -- We use the fact that the imaginary part of f is constant to show that f is constant.\n  -- Specifically, we use the property of complex numbers where constant imaginary parts imply constant functions.\n  apply Eq.symm\n  apply Eq.symm\n  -- We use the given property that the imaginary part of f is constant to conclude that f is constant.\n  simpa [ext_iff, hc] using hc a a"}
